// The case in two registers. First the decisions: the hero is the form that shipped cut to its
// first four rows, the deck is the ending, the snapshot four rows, then three chapters told as
// tension, decision, evidence, consequence, each frame a plain region of one of the Webflow
// page's own images (nothing painted out: the row-four dot and the green cursor mark stay).
// Then, past a ruled part opener, the record: everything the Webflow page kept — the two halves
// whole, the studio and its stakeholders, the workshops and the work map, the personas, the
// brief and the scale of the work, the components, every state of a write and of the editor's
// workflows, the light and dark monitors, the monitor state by state, the comparative test, the
// hand-off, the storyboard, the phones, the lessons — with that page's own images, the
// composites cut apart at their own panel edges and the transparent sheets flattened on white.
// The reader who stops after the decisions has the judgement; the one who keeps scrolling gets
// the whole summer.
---
<Case
  title="Eaton — Kyle Xu"
  description="UX Design Intern at Eaton, June–August 2022. RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems: a parameter editor and a fault monitor for airport engineers. In a comparative usability test the drag-and-drop editor was 35% faster and made 15% more errors, so the form shipped. 14 component variants added to Eaton's design system; in usability testing, operational efficiency +49.7% and error rates −36.8%. The decisions first, then the whole process: stakeholders, workshops, personas, 60 hi-fi pages over 10 workflows, the test, the hand-off, a mobile exploration."
  ogImage="/og-eaton.jpg"
  eyebrow="Eaton · RapidLinkX · UX Design Intern · June – August 2022"
  deck="The faster of the two editors we tested, 35% quicker, made 15% more errors, so the slower one shipped."
  next={{ title: 'Heygen', to: '/projects/heygen', line: 'The video editor, re-architected around the script.' }}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">A configuration tool for the drives that run an airport's conveyors.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" src={editor} evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    alt="RapidLinkX's parameter editor: a blue header and toolbar with a Real-Time switch turned off and a Write In button, linked drives listed on the left with one selected, and a table of four starred parameters with a value field, range, default and display checkbox on each row"
    caption="The slower one: a form with a row per parameter, its value beside its range and default." />

  <section class="col sec snap" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for the drives in airport conveyor systems, used by airport engineers: a parameter editor and a fault monitor, over USB. <a class="link" href="https://www.eaton.com/gb/en-gb/catalog/industrial-control--drives--automation---sensors/rapid-link-5.html" rel="noopener">The drives at Eaton ↗</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Role</dt>
        <dd>Primary designer on the <span class="nb">day-to-day</span> product design; my mentor on planning and <span class="nb">cross-team</span> communication.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Process</dt>
        <dd>Stakeholder workshops, secondary research and personas; more than 60 <span class="nb">hi-fi</span> pages over 10 workflows, in parallel with the hardware; a comparative usability test; six weeks of <span class="nb">hand-off</span>.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>Both shipped, with 14 component variants added to Eaton's Brightlayer design system. In usability testing of the shipped tool, operational efficiency was up 49.7% and error rates down 36.8%.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-editor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-editor">The slower editor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>A wrong value on a baggage line stops the line. The comparative usability test split its participants between a <span class="nb">drag-and-drop</span> canvas and this form: the canvas was quicker, and it made more errors, so it lost (it's under NDA, so it isn't shown).</p>
      <p>Two things gave the form part of its speed back. Starred keeps a check to the parameters that matter on a round. Compare puts the live value in one column and a saved configuration or the defaults in the next, so checking a drive is a read across each row.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="lead"
        items={[
          { src: starred, name: 'Starred', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'A round reads the starred rows, not the drive’s whole list.', alt: 'The Starred and All toggle with Starred selected, over the table’s header and its first row: a star, P-01, Motor Num Frequency, and a value field reading 50.0' },
          { src: compare, name: 'Compare', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'The live value beside the default: a drift is a read across the row.', alt: 'The comparison view: a breadcrumb reading Parameter Configuration, Parameter Comparison, the title Compare with default values, and a table header with one row, P-01 Motor Num Frequency, its current value, default and range' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-write">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-write">Writing to a drive</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>An engineer on a routine round wants the tool linked and watching. One at a drive that has just failed wants nothing written until they've decided what.</p>
      <p>So <span class="nb">real-time</span> writing is a switch, and it's off when the tool opens. Turning it on, or pressing Write In, brings up the same confirmation, with a backup of the drive's current configuration already ticked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure narrow src={confirm} name="The confirmation" evidence="Shipped"
        alt="The right of the editor's window: the Real-Time switch off at the toolbar's left, a Write In button at its right under a green cursor mark, the Starred and All toggle and the starred rows beneath, and a dialog over the table: Confirm Write-in, a note that changes will be written in real time to the connected inverter, a ticked checkbox to back up the config of the connected device, a config name reading DC 01, and Cancel and Confirm buttons"
        caption="Write In pressed with Real-Time off: the backup ticked before anything is written." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-monitor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-monitor">The monitor, in colour</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The same test set a <span class="nb">text-heavy</span> monitor against a <span class="nb">colour-coded</span> chart, and with the chart participants found errors 40% faster. The chart I drew first gave each of the four channels an axis of its own, which is fine until a spike on one needs reading against the other three. The shipped chart puts the four on one axis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure narrow set="stack"
        items={[
          { src: monitorDark, name: 'The draft', evidence: 'Concept', caption: 'Four axes, drawn dark: channel 4’s spike past 60 sits three bands below the channels it should be read against.', alt: 'The draft’s plot: four traces in blue, green, purple and orange, each on its own band of a dark grid with its own 0 to 80% scale, a legend floating over the top two, the orange trace spiking near 66 on the bottom band, and a time axis from 0 to 100 beneath' },
          { src: monitor, name: 'The monitor', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'One axis, and light, the Brightlayer theme the external developers were building from; the four colours adjusted for readability.', alt: 'The shipped plot: the same four colours on one white grid, the traces rising and falling over a hundred seconds, and a time axis from 0 to 100 beneath' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec part" aria-label="The record">
    <p class="meta">The record</p>
    <p class="sign">The three decisions above are the case. What follows is the rest of the summer as I recorded it at the time: how the studio worked, the research we could do, every state of the tool, the test, the hand-off, and what I took from it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-whole">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-whole">The two halves, whole</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Over the three months I designed RapidLinkX in two parts: a parameter editor for configuring a drive, and a monitor for finding and correcting faults. The work was stakeholder meetings, user research and prototyping, and it ran in parallel with the hardware.</p>
      <p>The editor sets a drive's motor speed, torque and <span class="nb">start/stop</span> settings, and its automatic operation modes and schedules. An engineer can save a set of parameters as a profile and switch between profiles, so one tool serves the different <span class="nb">material-handling</span> lines a drive ends up on. The monitor detects and diagnoses faults in real time, alerts the engineer, keeps error logs with corrective actions, and lets the configuration be adjusted so the fault doesn't come back.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack"
        items={[
          { src: editorWhole, name: 'The editor', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'Linked drives on the left, and a row per starred parameter on the right: its value, its range, its default, whether it’s displayed.', alt: 'The whole parameter editor: a blue header reading Rapid LinkX, a toolbar with new, open, save, share and revert icons, a Real-Time switch off and a Write In button, a sidebar listing a linked VFD 3001 in real time and the local configurations DC 02 to DC 09 under Airport A, and a table of twelve starred parameters, each P-01 Motor Num Frequency with a value of 50.0 or 0, a range of 10 to 500 Hz, a default of 50.0 Hz and a ticked display box' },
          { src: monitorWhole, name: 'The monitor', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'The linked drive and its recording settings on the left, eight channel slots across the top with four live, the four plotted over a hundred seconds, and a sixty-second overview beneath.', alt: 'The whole monitor: the linked drive VFD 3001 and its recording settings on the left, capture mode, time scaling, save files, log file name, logging interval and sample limit, a Start Recording button at the bottom; on the right the Waveform Recording and Error tabs, eight channel cards with the first four coloured blue, green, purple and orange and reading 12.4%, 20.3%, 39.2% and 2.3%, a plot of the four traces from 0 to 100 seconds, and a strip of the same traces over sixty seconds' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-studio">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-studio">The studio, and everyone the tool answered to</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>In 2022 I joined Eaton APAC's newly established <span class="nb">Human-Centered</span> Design department as one of its first UX interns, and was given a <span class="nb">high-priority</span> project: a <span class="nb">ground-up</span> design for a <span class="nb">parameter-editing</span> IoT product aimed at airport markets worldwide. Eaton, a global <span class="nb">power-management</span> company, was modernising its product experiences by pairing its business departments with the new design studio, Studio Blue, which worked like an internal design consultancy. The product team made the request, but the people with a say in it were an external development team, the <span class="nb">in-house</span> engineering and market teams, our direct clients and their secondary dealers, spread across the business, product development, sales and marketing teams of the APAC region. Before any design started, we had to find a way of working that would move the project through all of them.</p>
      <p>I was the primary designer on the <span class="nb">day-to-day</span> product design; my mentor's role was task planning and <span class="nb">cross-team</span> communication. Wanting more of that experience, I planned my own schedule and met her regularly to go over the collaboration, the research and the design. We set the strategy together, and I took the lead in carrying it out.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={stakeholders} name="The stakeholder map" evidence="Supporting state"
        alt="A map of two dashed circles: Eaton Corporation on the left holding the PM, the R&D team (Studio Blue), the presale support team, sales, the quality management team and the customer support team; Customer on the right holding the installer and the field engineer; between them a dealer, who is provided customers and sells the VFD, and a system integrator, who is provided VFD hardware and integrates solutions for customers; a line from presale support to the customer for training, deployment and commissioning, and one from customer support for commissioning and troubleshooting"
        caption="Eaton's teams on the left, the customer's installer and field engineer on the right, and between them the dealer and the system integrator who sell the drives and build them in." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-workshops">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-workshops">Workshops and the work map</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>To move the project we held several <span class="nb">design-thinking</span> workshops, so that partners and clients took part in the design decisions and understood every phase from R&amp;D to launch; it improved communication across all the teams. To make the plan clearer and easier to remember, my mentor and I took the conventional UX journey map and redrew it around our collaborative context and each stakeholder's role, which made it an organisational chart as much as a journey. The work map that came out of it outlines the whole process and tracks every stakeholder's interaction with the product and with sales across the product's lifecycle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={workMap} name="The work map" evidence="Supporting state"
        alt="A customer journey map in four stages, product presentation, training, parameter configuration and post-maintenance, with rows for who is involved (marketing, PMs, clients, pre-sales and after-sales engineers, client engineers), what is happening, the tools in use (slides, the product manual, Drive Connector, Excel, email and WeChat) and the issues: customers ask about parameters that are in the manual, a project may involve dozens of configurations, users struggle to capture precise fault information, only error codes are available"
        caption="Four stages, and under each who is involved, what happens, the tools in use and the issues: dozens of configurations per project, and faults reported as error codes alone." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-research">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-research">Research with limited resources</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Pandemic restrictions limited our access to experts, so we turned to online secondary research and to what the stakeholders told us in the workshop to understand the target audience. From that came detailed personas, which guided the design and, as much, improved communication inside the company: with the personas every team had the same picture of who the tool was for, and decisions were made against that picture rather than argued from each team's side.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={personas} name="The personas" evidence="Supporting state"
        alt="Two persona cards. Alan Smith, client field engineer, who executes, monitors, adjusts and reports: he specialises in parameter editing for airport machinery, often works at height and late, and wants replicable processes automated, timely remote support, supported error detection and analysis, and support for aerial work; his quote, I'm seeking a tool to automate parts of my workflow. Kevin Wang, Eaton application engineer, who deploys, coaches and supports: he gives remote technical support and software coaching to client engineers, and wants efficient information acquisition, remote-friendly experiences, automated guides built into the software, and cloud-based data; his quote, Clients and I found learning our apps challenging"
        caption="Alan, the client field engineer, wants parts of his workflow automated and support for work at height; Kevin, Eaton's application engineer, wants remote-friendly tools and cloud data." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-brief">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-brief">The brief, and the scale of the work</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The question we set ourselves: how might we create an intuitive tool that lets airport engineers detect hardware issues quickly and resolve them efficiently, minimising downtime and improving operational efficiency? The design goal under it: an IoT dashboard on which an airport engineer completes the parameter settings and performs the error checks for a conveyor system. The drive sits between the conveyor and the engineer's laptop, connected over USB.</p>
      <p>The UX design ran in parallel with the hardware's design and production. Our scrum workflow had a weekly or biweekly review with the hardware product team, so the design tracked the hardware as it changed, and we ordered the work by the importance of each workflow. More than 60 <span class="nb">hi-fi</span> pages were designed during the internship, of which 35 selected pages cover over 10 distinct workflows. Under Eaton's NDA, only the parts that have launched are shown here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack"
        items={[
          { src: system, name: 'The system', evidence: 'Supporting state', caption: 'The conveyor, the drives, the laptop: the tool runs on the laptop and talks to a drive over USB.', alt: 'Three pictures joined by a blue line: a photograph of an airport baggage conveyor system, two Eaton variable frequency drives, and a laptop, labelled conveyor system, hardware and laptop' },
          { src: board, name: 'The Figma board', evidence: 'Prototype', caption: 'The working file at the end of the summer: every flow laid out on one board, each under a coloured title bar.', alt: 'A zoomed-out Figma board: rows of small editor and monitor screens linked by arrows, each group under a coloured title bar in grey, blue, orange, green, purple, teal and yellow, with notes in Chinese' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-components">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-components">The core panel and the components</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The design starts from the fundamentals of how a page operates, how it's organised and how it's managed. On that base we customised specific components from Eaton's existing Brightlayer design system to present the data the tool needed, and what the system didn't have became 14 component variants added to it. The sheet below shows the ones around the drive list: the drive card in its states, the menu behind its three dots, and the dialogs a configuration passes through.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={components} name="Customised components" evidence="Shipped"
        alt="A sheet titled Customized Components: in a dashed box the drive card as a local configuration, plain and selected, as a linked VFD, plain and selected, and in real time, plain and with a tooltip reading Click The Menu Icon Or Right-Click; beneath it the card's menu, edit configuration name, copy configuration, restore to factory settings, delete; the editor in the middle; and on the right a notice, VFD detected, do you want to backup the configuration of the currently connected device, with an Export button, a Create dialog with a type and a project name, and a dialog reading Configuration already exists with Open and Overwrite"
        caption="The drive card local, linked and in real time, plain and selected; its right-click menu; and the notice, Create and Configuration already exists dialogs." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-ways">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-ways">Two ways to connect and write</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The tool connects to a drive in two ways, automatic and manual, for the different engineering situations our expert research described. Manual <span class="nb">write-in</span> gives the engineer control for routine maintenance and troubleshooting: when a drive on the baggage handling system fails without warning, the engineer connects by hand, goes straight to the diagnostics and the troubleshooting features, and resolves it there. Auto connect is for continuous monitoring and automated processes: on routine work such as watching the whole conveyor system, the tool keeps checking and raises an alert on any irregularity or likely failure without the engineer having to step in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <div class="two">
        <Figure set="stack"
          items={[
            { src: autoSelect, name: 'Auto connect', note: 'selection', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'The Real-Time switch turned on: a dialog says changes will be written to the drive as they’re made, and offers the backup first.', alt: 'The editor with a cursor on the Real-Time switch, now green, and a dialog: Enabling Real-time Writing Mode, any changes you make will be written in real time to the connected inverter, a ticked box to back up the config of the connected device, a config name reading DC 01, Cancel and Confirm' },
            { src: autoLinked, name: 'Auto connect', note: 'linking', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'Linked in real time: the drive card turns green and reads REALTIME, and a progress bar runs under the toolbar.', alt: 'The editor linked in real time: the VFD 3001 card at the top of the sidebar is green and labelled REALTIME, a green progress bar runs under the toolbar, and the starred parameters fill the table' },
            { src: manualSelect, name: 'Manual write-in', note: 'selection', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'Write In pressed with Real-Time off: the same confirmation, the backup ticked.', alt: 'The editor with a green cursor mark on the Write In button and the Confirm Write-in dialog open over the table, with the backup box ticked and a config name reading DC 01' },
            { src: manualAction, name: 'Manual write-in', note: 'action', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'Writing: a notice at the top right asks not to disconnect, with Cancel beside it.', alt: 'The editor while a write runs: a notice at the top right reads Writing to device, please do not disconnect, with a Cancel button' },
          ]} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-workflows">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-workflows">The workflows around the editor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>We refined several workflows and display modes to shorten the path through a parameter setting and take steps out of it.</p>
      <ul class="terms">
        <li><b>Empty state.</b> The tool opens on the most recent files, so an engineer is back in the last configuration straight away.</li>
        <li><b>Routine options.</b> Presets of parameters can be created and saved, then imported into a drive in one step.</li>
        <li><b>Comparison mode.</b> A <span class="nb">side-by-side</span> of a past dataset and the current one, for analysis and for working out a fix.</li>
        <li><b>Favourites.</b> The parameters that matter most on a configuration check are starred, so the critical settings are always in reach.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <div class="two">
        <Figure set="stack"
          items={[
            { src: empty, name: 'Empty state', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'Nothing connected: a prompt to connect an inverter over USB, and the recently opened configurations beneath.', alt: 'The editor with no drive connected: a file icon, the text Connect the inverter to your local configuration via USB, a note that parameters can be edited offline, an Open Local Configs button, and four recently opened DX-COM-STICK default settings files' },
            { src: routine, name: 'Routine options', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'Creating a configuration: a new project, then a configuration saved into it.', alt: 'Three Create dialogs in a row over a dimmed editor: the first with a type of New project and a project name; the second with a config name and a ticked box to save the configuration to a project; the third with a type of New Config and a project chosen from a list' },
            { src: comparison, name: 'Comparison mode', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'Compare with default values, and the dialog for choosing what to compare against: an open configuration or a local file.', alt: 'The comparison page, Compare with default values: a table of parameters P-01 to P-14 with current value, default and range, and a dialog, Select comparison target, listing Open a local file and Configuration 001, 002 and 003' },
            { src: favorites, name: 'Favourites', note: 'star', evidence: 'Shipped', caption: 'All parameters, in nine categories, beside the Starred view; 3,456 of them in this drive.', alt: 'The editor on its All view, a cursor on the All toggle: a row of chips, Param Cat 1 to Param Cat 9, above the table, and a footer reading 1 to 10 of 3,456' },
          ]} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-modes">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-modes">The monitor, light or dark</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Besides setting parameters, engineers need to watch the data to find errors quickly and accurately, for a fix later on. So two versions of the monitor were drawn. Dark mode reads better, but the light version was chosen as the final design because it fits the overall design system; the design system's colour scheme was adjusted slightly to improve readability.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="lead"
        items={[
          { src: dark, name: 'Dark', evidence: 'Concept', dim: true, caption: 'Better to read, and off the system.', alt: 'The monitor drawn dark: four channel cards across the top, four traces each on its own band of a dark grid, a legend of the four channels floating at the right, and an overview strip beneath' },
          { src: light, name: 'Light', note: 'chosen for iteration', evidence: 'Prototype', caption: 'On the system, taken forward; the labels still in Chinese at this stage.', alt: 'The same monitor drawn light: four channel cards, four traces each on its own band of a white grid, and an overview strip beneath, with the buttons labelled in Chinese' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-states">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-states">The monitor, state by state</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>With the version chosen, we turned to the functions that matter for operational efficiency: a close reading of the workflow for bottlenecks, and priority to whatever took manual steps out and made the state of the system easier to see.</p>
      <ul class="terms">
        <li><b>Error reports.</b> Alert the engineer to a malfunction or a discrepancy, with a detailed breakdown and possible solutions.</li>
        <li><b>Empty state.</b> When no file is open or a session starts, it leads straight to setting the monitored channel.</li>
        <li><b>Normal view.</b> A <span class="nb">real-time</span> overview of the system, so the engineer keeps a constant read on current states and parameters without opening deeper views.</li>
        <li><b>Recording.</b> Records the live data stream for detailed review and historical analysis; what lets an engineer trace the events before an error.</li>
        <li><b>Exports.</b> Data and reports in several formats, to share across departments or with external stakeholders.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <div class="two">
